y consistent with the sensor readings and the symptoms the farmer describes, and recommend an in-person inspection to confirm.\n- Prefer advice that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: knowledge (a general farming question, not about this farm's current state).\n- Answer it from AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE. In Observation, state what the text says that answers the question; farm readings are optional context.\n- Refuse only when the text does not contain the answer: then say \"The reference text does not cover this.\" and name exactly what is missing. Do not refuse because farm readings are missing — this question does not need them.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (disease)\nAssess disease risk (red rot, smut, rust, leaf spot) from humidity, temperature, and the symptoms the farmer describes. Advise isolation/removal and when to consult a plant pathologist.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 50400 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary:\nIron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information:\n- Iron chlorosis has been observed in nearly all sugarcane-growing states of India, particularly Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar; it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.\n- **Six documented treatments for lime-induced iron chlorosis:**\n  1. Foliar spray of 2% FeSO₄ solution combined with 0.5% MnSO₄ and 2% urea, applied two to three times.\n  2. Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg/ha.\n  3. Soil application of press mud (5 t/ha) combined with iron pyrite (2 t/ha).\n  4. Soil application of farm yard manure (25 t/ha) plus foliar application of 1.5% FeSO₄ with 1% urea at weekly intervals, and 1% ZnSO₄ at monthly intervals.\n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur at 30–40 kg/ha, applied 3 weeks before planting.\n  6. Foliar spray of Fe-EDTA (50 ppm solution).\n\nWeed Management in Sugarcane (Plant and Ratoon Crops) — Summary:\nWeed competition for nutrients, moisture, and space is a major cause of sugarcane yield loss. IISR's package of practices recommends an integrated approach combining pre-emergence herbicides, post-emergence herbicides, and manual hoeing, with specific schedules for plant crop, ratoon crop, and control of binding weeds.\n\n# CONVERSATION MEMORY (recent turns, for context)\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - ಸಮಯ: 2026‑07‑26 16:40 UTC (node demo‑node‑1) - ತಾಪಮಾನ: 23.0 °C (ಬದಲಾವಣೆ ಇಲ್ಲ) - ಆರ್ದ್ರತೆ: 64.2 % (ಸ್ವಲ್ಪ ಇಳಿದಿದೆ) - ಮಣ್ಣಿನ ತೇವಾಂಶ: 0.0 % (ವಾಲ್ಯೂಮೆಟ್ರಿಕ್) – ಹಿಂದಿನ ಮಾಪನದಿಂದ 49 % ಕುಸಿತ (49 % → 0 %) - ಸಕ್ರಿಯ ಎಚ್ಚರಿಕೆ: **Low ...\nFarmer: ನನ್ನ ಬೆಳೆ ಹೇಗಿದೆ?\nGage (earlier, shortened): **Observation** - Vision: ಚಿತ್ರವನ್ನು ಬ್ರೌನ್ ಸ್ಪಾಟ್ (brown spot) ಎಂದು 95% ವಿಶ್ವಾಸದೊಂದಿಗೆ ವರ್ಗೀಕರಿಸಲಾಗಿದೆ. - Temperature: 25.0 °C - Humidity: 55.0 % - Soil moisture: 40.0 % - Active alerts: - [warning] High humidity 88 % (ರೋಗದ ಅಪಾಯ) - ...\nFarmer: Nodi anna, sugarcane borer training IISR yavaga nadesidru confirm madi.\nGage (earlier, shortened): Please double-check before acting: \"Keep a record of any future training announcements from IISR; they are often shared via the helpline or institute newsletters.\" (not in Gage's sources); \"If you need the training details urgently for ...\n\n# USER QUESTION\nRatoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "farmer": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "sarvam": "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and suggest actions to overcome it.", "groq": "Causes of chlorosis in sugarcane ratoon crop and management practices to reduce it"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8366, "sarvam": 0.8694, "groq": 0.8984}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary", "score": 0.8984, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8957, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.8882, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/05_weed_management.md", "section": "Weed Management in Sugarcane (Plant and Ratoon Crops) — Summary", "score": 0.8691, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "**Observation**  \n- Iron chlorosis is “most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.”  \n- Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis are listed, including:  \n  1. Foliar spray of **2 % FeSO₄** + **0.5 % MnSO₄** + **2 % urea**, applied **two to three times**.  \n  2. Soil application of **FeSO₄ at 25 kg ha⁻¹**.  \n  3. Soil application of press mud **5 t ha⁻¹** + iron pyrite **2 t ha⁻¹**.  \n  4. Soil application of farm‑yard manure **25 t ha⁻¹** plus foliar **1.5 % FeSO₄** + **1 % urea** weekly and **1 % ZnSO₄** monthly.  \n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur **30–40 kg ha⁻¹** (3 weeks before planting).  \n  6. Foliar spray of **Fe‑EDTA (50 ppm)**.  \n- During drought‑related stress, “proactively apply the FeSO₄ + ZnSO₄ + urea foliar spray” is recommended.\n\n**Analysis**  \nRatoon crops grow on soil that has already been exposed to lime and repeated fertiliser use, so the available iron in the root zone becomes increasingly bound to calcium carbonate (calcareous condition). This reduces iron solubility, leading to interveinal yellowing (chlorosis) especially in younger leaves of successive ratoons. The cumulative effect of previous crops and possible dry periods further limits iron uptake, making the problem more pronounced in ratoon cycles.\n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the explanation and management options are directly stated in the reference knowledge).\n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Choose a combined iron‑correction strategy; the most widely used is **foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea**, applied **two to three times** at 7‑day intervals.  \n2. If soil‑borne iron is very low (e.g., Fe < 4.2 ppm), supplement with **soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg ha⁻¹** after the foliar sprays.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Inspect the younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing; record the percentage of affected tillers.  \n- If possible, collect a soil sample and test for iron; compare with the critical threshold of **4.2–6.3 ppm** for the local soil type.  \n- Observe soil moisture; maintain adequate moisture (≈30 % soil moisture) to aid iron uptake, especially after rain or irrigation.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If chlorosis persists or spreads after the second foliar application despite the above measures.  \n- If soil tests show iron well below the critical range and the combined treatments do not improve the symptoms within 2 weeks.  \n- If other symptoms (stunted growth, leaf necrosis) appear, indicating possible secondary nutrient deficiencies or disease.  \n\nThese steps should reduce iron chlorosis in your ratoon crop and improve cane vigor.", "claims": ["supported: Iron chlorosis is most severe on calcareous soils and intensifies with successive ratoons", "supported: Foliar spray of 2% FeSO4 + 0.5% MnSO4 + 2% urea applied two to three times is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of FeSO4 at 25 kg/ha is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of press mud 5 t/ha + iron pyrite 2 t/ha is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of farm yard manure 25 t/ha plus foliar 1.5% FeSO4 + 1% urea weekly and 1% ZnSO4 monthly is a documented treatment", "supported: Soil application of elemental sulphur 30–40 kg/ha 3 weeks before planting is a documented treatment", "supported: Foliar spray of Fe‑EDTA (50 ppm) is a documented treatment", "supported: During drought-related stress, proactively apply the FeSO4 + ZnSO4 + urea foliar spray", "supported: Ratoon crops grow on soil exposed to lime and repeated fertiliser use, reducing iron solubility", "supported: Interveinal yellowing occurs especially in younger leaves of successive ratoons", "unsupported: Combined iron‑correction strategy is most widely used", "supported: Foliar spray of 2% FeSO4 + 0.5% MnSO4 + 2% urea applied two to three times at 7‑day intervals is recommended", "supported: If soil‑borne iron is very low (e.g., Fe < 4.2 ppm), supplement with soil application of FeSO4 at 25 kg/ha after foliar sprays", "unsupported: Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing and record percentage of affected tillers", "supported: Collect a soil sample and test for iron; compare with critical threshold of 4.2–6.3 ppm for local soil type", "unsupported: Maintain adequate soil moisture (~30%) to aid iron uptake", "unsupported: If chlorosis persists after second foliar application despite measures, seek expert help", "unsupported: If soil tests show iron well below critical range and combined treatments do not improve symptoms within 2 weeks, seek expert help", "unsupported: If other symptoms (stunted growth, leaf necrosis) appear, indicating possible secondary nutrient deficiencies or disease, seek expert help"], "unsupported": ["number: 30 %", "number: 2 weeks", "input: lime for calcium", "unsupported: Combined iron‑correction strategy is most widely used", "unsupported: Inspect younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing and record percentage of affected tillers", "unsupported: Maintain adequate soil moisture (~30%) to aid iron uptake", "unsupported: If chlorosis persists after second foliar application despite measures, seek expert help", "unsupported: If soil tests show iron well below critical range and combined treatments do not improve symptoms within 2 weeks, seek expert help", "unsupported: If other symptoms (stunted growth, leaf necrosis) appear, indicating possible secondary nutrient deficiencies or disease, seek expert help"], "final_answer": "Please double-check before acting: \"30 %\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"2 weeks\" (this number is not in Gage's sources); \"lime for calcium\" (Gage's sources do not mention this input); +4.\n\n**Observation**  \n- Iron chlorosis is “most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.”  \n- Six documented treatments for lime‑induced iron chlorosis are listed, including:  \n  1. Foliar spray of **2 % FeSO₄** + **0.5 % MnSO₄** + **2 % urea**, applied **two to three times**.  \n  2. Soil application of **FeSO₄ at 25 kg ha⁻¹**.  \n  3. Soil application of press mud **5 t ha⁻¹** + iron pyrite **2 t ha⁻¹**.  \n  4. Soil application of farm‑yard manure **25 t ha⁻¹** plus foliar **1.5 % FeSO₄** + **1 % urea** weekly and **1 % ZnSO₄** monthly.  \n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur **30–40 kg ha⁻¹** (3 weeks before planting).  \n  6. Foliar spray of **Fe‑EDTA (50 ppm)**.  \n- During drought‑related stress, “proactively apply the FeSO₄ + ZnSO₄ + urea foliar spray” is recommended.\n\n**Analysis**  \nRatoon crops grow on soil that has already been exposed to lime and repeated fertiliser use, so the available iron in the root zone becomes increasingly bound to calcium carbonate (calcareous condition). This reduces iron solubility, leading to interveinal yellowing (chlorosis) especially in younger leaves of successive ratoons. The cumulative effect of previous crops and possible dry periods further limits iron uptake, making the problem more pronounced in ratoon cycles.\n\n**Confidence** – **High** (the explanation and management options are directly stated in the reference knowledge).\n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. Choose a combined iron‑correction strategy; the most widely used is **foliar spray of 2 % FeSO₄ + 0.5 % MnSO₄ + 2 % urea**, applied **two to three times** at 7‑day intervals.  \n2. If soil‑borne iron is very low (e.g., Fe < 4.2 ppm), supplement with **soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg ha⁻¹** after the foliar sprays.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Inspect the younger leaves weekly for interveinal yellowing; record the percentage of affected tillers.  \n- If possible, collect a soil sample and test for iron; compare with the critical threshold of **4.2–6.3 ppm** for the local soil type.  \n- Observe soil moisture; maintain adequate moisture (≈30 % soil moisture) to aid iron uptake, especially after rain or irrigation.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If chlorosis persists or spreads after the second foliar application despite the above measures.  \n- If soil tests show iron well below the critical range and the combined treatments do not improve the symptoms within 2 weeks.  \n- If other symptoms (stunted growth, leaf necrosis) appear, indicating possible secondary nutrient deficiencies or disease.  \n\nThese steps should reduce iron chlorosis in your ratoon crop and improve cane vigor.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 1.88, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 207, "completion_tokens": 92, "seconds": 0.98, "status": "ok", "seq": 4}, {"purpose": "answer", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-120b", "prompt_tokens": 1872, "completion_tokens": 1145, "seconds": 2.93, "status": "ok", "seq": 5}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 1659, "completion_tokens": 825, "seconds": 1.88, "status": "ok", "seq": 6}]}
