ce that relies on the farm's own observations, not external weather.\n- Reply in the farmer's language: Kannada if they wrote Kannada, else English.\n\nQUESTION TYPE: farm (about this farm now).\n- Use the farm's sensor readings, alerts and history as the Observed Facts, and AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE for what they mean.\n- If the readings are missing, old or not enough to answer, still say what they do show, then name exactly what is missing (for example a soil-moisture reading, the crop stage, a description of the symptom). Use Confidence Low in that case. Do not refuse outright.\n\n# FOCUS FOR THIS QUESTION (general)\nAnswer the farmer's question from the knowledge text, using this farm's data where it is relevant.\n\n# FARM\nName: Demo Farm\nCrop: sugarcane\nLocation: Mandya\nFarmer: Demo Farmer\nToday: 05 October 2026\n\n# CURRENT OBSERVATION\n- Time: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC (node demo-node-1)\n- Sensor values: as in SENSOR READINGS below\n- GPS: 12.806987279716143, 74.93193321230041\n\n# SENSOR READINGS (latest)\n- Taken: 2026-09-23 04:25 UTC\n- Temperature: 27.9 C\n- Humidity: 72.4 %\n- Soil moisture: 32.3 %\n\n# RECENT HISTORY\nOnly one observation on record — no comparison possible yet.\nObservations on record: 10 (most recent first).\n\n# ACTIVE ALERTS\nAddress these FIRST:\n- [critical] Node offline (no contact for 50400 min)\n\n# AGRICULTURAL KNOWLEDGE\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations:\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nNutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary:\nIron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\nSugarcane Disease Management — Summary:\nSugarcane in India is affected by several economically damaging diseases — red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot disease, ratoon stunting disease, yellow leaf disease, pokkah boeng, and rust. IISR documents the causal organism and specific management approach for each, with an overarching emphasis on resistant varieties, sett/seed health, and hot-water/heat treatment as the primary defence.\n\nSugarcane Disease Management — Important Recommendations:\n- Because most of these diseases (red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot, ratoon stunting, yellow leaf) are seed/sett-transmitted, the single highest-leverage preventive action for farmers is sourcing certified disease-free planting material (ideally tissue-culture or nursery-derived, per the Planting Methods document) rather than relying on curative treatment after symptoms appear.\n- Hot water treatment at 50–52°C for 2–3 hours is a recurring, low-cost control measure across multiple diseases (smut, grassy shoot, ratoon stunting) and should be a standard nursery practice before establishing new seed material.\n- For fungal foliar diseases (pokkah boeng, rust), timely fungicide spraying at first symptom appearance, r