ows a moderate soil‑moisture level (**32.3 %**); if moisture drops further during the dry spell, iron becomes less available, a known secondary effect.  \n- Without a recent soil‑test we cannot confirm the exact Fe concentration, but the critical threshold for iron is **below 4.2–6.3 ppm** (depending on soil type).  \n- Therefore the most likely cause is an iron (or broader micronutrient) deficiency, possibly aggravated by limited moisture.  \n\n**Confidence** – **Medium** – because the symptom matches iron chlorosis, but we lack a soil‑test result and detailed symptom description.  \n\n**Recommendations**  \n\n*Immediate actions*  \n1. **Apply a combined foliar spray** of FeSO₄ + ZnSO₄ + urea (as recommended for drought‑related iron deficiency). Follow label rates for sugarcane (typically 2 L ha⁻¹ of a 0.5 % FeSO₄ solution).  \n2. **Check irrigation**: ensure the soil moisture stays above the current **32.3 %** level; add water if it begins to fall, aiming for a consistent moisture profile.  \n3. **Address the node alert**: inspect the sensor node for power or connectivity issues; replace battery or reset the modem so future readings are available.  \n\n*Monitoring advice*  \n- Observe new leaf growth for improvement in color over the next 7‑10 days.  \n- Re‑measure soil moisture weekly; keep it stable or slightly higher than the current **32.3 %** during dry periods.  \n- Arrange a **soil test** (especially for Fe, Zn, Mn, Cu) within the next two weeks; compare results to the critical **4.2–6.3 ppm** Fe threshold.  \n\n*When to seek expert help*  \n- If yellowing spreads to the entire canopy or new growth remains yellow after the foliar spray.  \n- If the soil test shows Fe < 4.2 ppm or other micronutrient deficiencies.  \n- If the sensor node remains offline despite troubleshooting, or if you notice additional symptoms (e.g., stunted shoots, wilting).  \n\nPromptly implementing the foliar spray and confirming soil micronutrient status will usually resolve iron‑re