2026-10-05 13:42:12,286 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "provider": "sarvam_finetuned", "answer_seconds": 10.74, "language": "en", "question": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "retrieval_queries": ["Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and suggest actions to overcome it.", "Causes of chlorosis in sugarcane ratoon crop and management practices to reduce it"], "model_input": {"context": "Iron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\n- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\n- Iron chlorosis has been observed in nearly all sugarcane-growing states of India, particularly Madhya Pradesh, Maharashtra, Tamil Nadu, and Bihar; it is most severe on calcareous soils and tends to intensify with successive ratoons.\n- **Six documented treatments for lime-induced iron chlorosis:**\n  1. Foliar spray of 2% FeSO₄ solution combined with 0.5% MnSO₄ and 2% urea, applied two to three times.\n  2. Soil application of FeSO₄ at 25 kg/ha.\n  3. Soil application of press mud (5 t/ha) combined with iron pyrite (2 t/ha).\n  4. Soil application of farm yard manure (25 t/ha) plus foliar application of 1.5% FeSO₄ with 1% urea at weekly intervals, and 1% ZnSO₄ at monthly intervals.\n  5. Soil application of elemental sulphur at 30–40 kg/ha, applied 3 weeks before planting.\n  6. Foliar spray of Fe-EDTA (50 ppm solution).\n\nWeed competition for nutrients, moisture, and space is a major cause of sugarcane yield loss. IISR's package of practices recommends an integrated approach combining pre-emergence herbicides, post-emergence herbicides, and manual hoeing, with specific schedules for plant crop, ratoon crop, and control of binding weeds.", "farmer": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Ratoon crop nalli chlorosis yaake jaasti aaguttade yenu madbeku heli.", "sarvam": "Cause of chlorosis in rotted crop and suggest actions to overcome it.", "groq": "Causes of chlorosis in sugarcane ratoon crop and management practices to reduce it"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8366, "sarvam": 0.8694, "groq": 0.8984}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary", "score": 0.8984, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8957, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.8882, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/05_weed_management.md", "section": "Weed Management in Sugarcane (Plant and Ratoon Crops) — Summary", "score": 0.8691, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "Anna, Ratoon crop matte barthirodhu kondu chintisidre, Chlorosis Symptoms ge yeshtu important -- Interveen leafy young leaves symptoms irabeku -- follow maadi.", "claims": ["supported: Ratoon crops cause more chlorosis", "supported: Chlorosis symptoms include interveinal yellowing of younger leaves"], "unsupported": [], "final_answer": "Anna, Ratoon crop matte barthirodhu kondu chintisidre, Chlorosis Symptoms ge yeshtu important -- Interveen leafy young leaves symptoms irabeku -- follow maadi.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 0.85, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 207, "completion_tokens": 92, "seconds": 0.59, "status": "ok", "seq": 37}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 834, "completion_tokens": 221, "seconds": 0.85, "status": "ok", "seq": 38}]}
