2026-10-05 13:45:31,252 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "provider": "sarvam_finetuned", "answer_seconds": 7.95, "language": "kn", "question": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "retrieval_queries": ["ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "I am confused about which sugarcane variety to grow and how to get the right variety.", "How to choose the best sugarcane variety for my farm conditions?"], "model_input": {"context": "- **Tissue culture**: enables rapid multiplication of high-yielding, disease-resistant varieties; conventional vegetative propagation requires large seed quantities and has a low multiplication rate (10-12 years from variety release to field-scale coverage); tissue culture can compress this multiplication timeline to about 5 years and produces disease-free plantlets from apical meristem tissue.\n- **Spaced Transplanting (STP)**: raised nursery beds (3 ft wide) are prepared with FYM; single-bud setts are dipped in 0.2% Carbendazim, planted vertically, and treated with chlorpyriphos or imidacloprid against pests. Settlings are transplanted after 6–7 weeks, achieving 90–95% survival with careful management. Seed cane requirement drops to ~20 quintals/ha versus ~60 quintals/ha for normal planting.\n\n- Farmers should select varieties matched to their specific agro-climatic zone and planting season (early vs mid-late vs late) rather than using a single variety across all conditions, to balance cane tonnage with sugar recovery.\n- In Karnataka and other Adsali-planting tropical states, farmers with adequate water availability can consider Adsali (July–August) planting for higher yield and sugar recovery, provided irrigation water is not a limiting constraint.\n- Because sugarcane occupies land for 12–18 months, crop diversification through intercropping or sequential cropping (e.g., paddy–sugarcane–ratoon–finger millet/sesamum/blackgram systems, which are documented for Karnataka's peninsular zone) is recommended to generate mid-season income and reduce monoculture risk.\n\n- Even under satisfactory conditions, only about 40–45% of buds on planted setts typically germinate, making seed health and treatment central to improving stand establishment.\n- **Seed rate and spacing vary by region.** For Karnataka (tropical/peninsular zone): recommended seed rate is 25,000–30,000 three-bud setts per hectare, with row spacing of about 90 cm. For comparison, Tamil Nadu uses 42,000–45,000 setts/ha at 80 cm spacing, and subtropical Uttar Pradesh uses 35,000 setts/ha (timely planting, 90 cm spacing) or up to 56,000 setts/ha for late planting at 60 cm spacing.\n- **Seed treatment recommendations:**\n  1. Always use disease-free, quality setts for planting.\n  2. To protect against termites and ants, treat seed cane/stalk with chlorpyriphos at 1.0 kg a.i./ha.\n\n- Use only disease-free nursery-derived setts, ideally sourced via advanced multiplication techniques (see Planting Methods document for nursery/bud-chip techniques) rather than random field cane, to minimize sett-borne disease carryover (red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot disease, ratoon stunting disease).\n- Match seed rate and spacing to the specific agro-climatic zone and planting season — Karnataka farmers should use 25,000–30,000 setts/ha at ~90 cm spacing under normal conditions, adjusting for late planting or drought-prone light soils.\n- Combine chemical seed treatment (fungicide + insecticide) with bio-inoculant treatment for both disease/pest protection and improved nutrient-use efficiency.", "farmer": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ."}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "ನಾನು ಯಾವ ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಹಾಕೊಬೇಕು ಅಂತ confuse ಆಗಿದೀನಿ, ಸರಿಯಾದ variety ಹೇಗೆ ಆರಿಸ್ಕೊಳಿ ಅಂತ ಹೇಳಿ.", "sarvam": "I am confused about which sugarcane variety to grow and how to get the right variety.", "groq": "How to choose the best sugarcane variety for my farm conditions?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8223, "sarvam": 0.8755, "groq": 0.8692}, "retrieved": [{"source": "sugarcane/04_planting_methods_and_nursery_techniques.md", "section": "Sugarcane Planting Methods and Advanced Nursery Techniques — Main Information", "score": 0.8755, "via": "sarvam"}, {"source": "sugarcane/01_sugarcane_cultivation_overview_india_and_karnataka.md", "section": "Sugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.863, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/03_seed_selection_and_seed_treatment.md", "section": "Sugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Main Information", "score": 0.8692, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/03_seed_selection_and_seed_treatment.md", "section": "Sugarcane Seed Selection, Seed Rate, and Seed Treatment — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8672, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "ಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡುವಾಗ, ನಿಮ್ಮ specific agro-climatic zone ಮತ್ತು planting season ಗೆ match ಆಗೋ ಹಾಗೆ ನೋಡಿ, ಯಾಕೆಂದರೆ ಒಂದೇ variety ಎಲ್ಲಾ conditions ಅಲ್ಲೂ work ಮಾಡಲ್ಲ.", "claims": ["unsupported: A sugarcane variety should match the farmer's specific agro-climatic zone and planting season, because one variety does not work in all conditions."], "unsupported": ["unsupported: A sugarcane variety should match the farmer's specific agro-climatic zone and planting season, because one variety does not work in all conditions."], "final_answer": "ದಯವಿಟ್ಟು ಮುಂದುವರಿಯುವ ಮೊದಲು ಪರಿಶೀಲಿಸಿ: \"A sugarcane variety should match the farmer's specific agro-climatic zone and planting season, because one variety does not work in all conditions.\" (Gage ಮಾಹಿತಿ ದಾಖಲೆಗಳಲ್ಲಿ ಇಲ್ಲ).\n\nಕಬ್ಬಿನ ತಳಿ ಆಯ್ಕೆ ಮಾಡುವಾಗ, ನಿಮ್ಮ specific agro-climatic zone ಮತ್ತು planting season ಗೆ match ಆಗೋ ಹಾಗೆ ನೋಡಿ, ಯಾಕೆಂದರೆ ಒಂದೇ variety ಎಲ್ಲಾ conditions ಅಲ್ಲೂ work ಮಾಡಲ್ಲ.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 1.31, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite",