2026-10-05 13:47:57,704 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "provider": "sarvam_finetuned", "answer_seconds": 16.15, "language": "en", "question": "Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?", "retrieval_queries": ["Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?", "yellow leaves sugarcane nutrient deficiency or disease causes"], "model_input": {"context": "- Farmers on calcareous or high-pH soils (common in parts of Karnataka's black-soil tracts) should watch for chlorosis symptoms (interveinal yellowing of younger leaves) especially in ratoon crops, and apply combined foliar + soil iron treatment rather than a single application, since the source material documents six distinct combined strategies rather than one standalone fix.\n- Soil testing against the documented critical thresholds (e.g., Fe below 4.2–6.3 ppm depending on soil type) should guide whether a general basal micronutrient mix or a targeted single-nutrient correction is warranted.\n- During drought-related contingency management, proactively apply the FeSO₄+ZnSO₄+urea foliar spray rather than waiting for visible chlorosis symptoms, since iron deficiency is a known secondary effect of extended dry spells.\n\nIron chlorosis is the most widely occurring nutritional deficiency in sugarcane, especially on calcareous soils, and worsens in successive ratoon crops. IISR documents six treatment options for chlorosis management and identifies the broader set of micronutrients (B, Cu, Fe, S, Mg, Mn, Mo, Zn) affecting cane growth, along with critical soil deficiency thresholds.\n\nSugarcane in India is affected by several economically damaging diseases — red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot disease, ratoon stunting disease, yellow leaf disease, pokkah boeng, and rust. IISR documents the causal organism and specific management approach for each, with an overarching emphasis on resistant varieties, sett/seed health, and hot-water/heat treatment as the primary defence.\n\n- Because most of these diseases (red rot, smut, wilt, grassy shoot, ratoon stunting, yellow leaf) are seed/sett-transmitted, the single highest-leverage preventive action for farmers is sourcing certified disease-free planting material (ideally tissue-culture or nursery-derived, per the Planting Methods document) rather than relying on curative treatment after symptoms appear.\n- Hot water treatment at 50–52°C for 2–3 hours is a recurring, low-cost control measure across multiple diseases (smut, grassy shoot, ratoon stunting) and should be a standard nursery practice before establishing new seed material.\n- For fungal foliar diseases (pokkah boeng, rust), timely fungicide spraying at first symptom appearance, repeated at 15-day intervals, is more effective than a single late-stage application.", "farmer": "Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Why are the leaves of my cane turning yellow?", "groq": "yellow leaves sugarcane nutrient deficiency or disease causes"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.847, "groq": 0.896}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8933, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Summary", "score": 0.896, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "diseases/01_disease_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Disease Management — Summary", "score": 0.8903, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "diseases/01_disease_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Disease Management — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8879, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "Answer: Chlorosis due to prolonged dry weather conditions during crop recovery phase; this would affect both sucrose accumulation and nutrient uptake efficiency because moisture stress reduces photosynthetic activity which affects carbohydrate synthesis & translocation within plant tissues leading to reduced sugar formation coupled with poor nutrient assimilation resulting in lower yield potential.", "claims": ["unsupported: Chlorosis due to prolonged dry weather conditions during crop recovery phase", "unsupported: Moisture stress reduces photosynthetic activity which affects carbohydrate synthesis and translocation within plant tissues leading to reduced sugar formation coupled with poor nutrient assimilation resulting in lower yield potential"], "unsupported": ["unsupported: Chlorosis due to prolonged dry weather conditions during crop recovery phase", "unsupported: Moisture stress reduces photosynthetic activity which affects carbohydrate synthesis and translocation within plant tissues leading to reduced sugar formation coupled with poor nutrient assimilation resulting in lower yield potential"], "final_answer": "Please double-check before acting: \"Chlorosis due to prolonged dry weather conditions during crop recovery phase\" (not in Gage's sources); \"Moisture stress reduces photosynthetic activity which affects carbohydrate synthesis and translocation within plant tissues leading to reduced sugar formation coupled with poor nutrient assimilation resulting in lower yield potential\" (not in Gage's sources).\n\nAnswer: Chlorosis due to prolonged dry weather conditions during crop recovery phase; this would affect both sucrose accumulation and nutrient uptake efficiency because moisture stress reduces photosynthetic activity which affects carbohydrate synthesis & translocation within plant tissues leading to reduced sugar formation coupled with poor nutrient assimilation resulting in lower yield potential.", "fact_check": "checked", "fact_check_seconds": 2.03, "groq_calls": [{"purpose": "rewrite", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 195, "completion_tokens": 40, "seconds": 0.58, "status": "ok", "seq": 50}, {"purpose": "fact_check", "model": "openai/gpt-oss-20b", "prompt_tokens": 854, "completion_tokens": 241, "seconds": 2.03, "status": "ok", "seq": 51}]}
