2026-10-05 13:48:49,693 gage.orchestrator INFO answer trace {"farm": 1, "provider": "sarvam_finetuned", "answer_seconds": 16.81, "language": "en", "question": "Is my soil too dry right now?", "retrieval_queries": ["Is my soil too dry right now?", "Is my soil too dry?", "soil moisture level sugarcane dry?"], "model_input": {"context": "- Under drought conditions, contingency guidance also recommends a foliar spray of 1% FeSO₄ + 0.5% ZnSO₄ + 2% urea when iron deficiency is expected due to prolonged dry spells (6–8 weeks delayed monsoon).\n\n- Sugarcane requires a mean temperature of 28–32°C for optimal growth; temperatures above 45°C reduce tillering, and below 20°C slow growth. Areas with minimum temperature under 5°C are unsuitable.\n- The crop needs 75–120 cm of annual rainfall (if unirrigated) and a long growing season of 10–18 months. Ideal relative humidity is 70–85% during growth and 55–75% during ripening; below 50% humidity during growth is unsuitable.\n- India has two broad sugarcane regions:\n  - **Tropical region** (Maharashtra, Karnataka, Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Madhya Pradesh, Kerala): ~45% of area, 55% of national production; productivity ~80 t/ha.\n\n- Sucrose loss window: deterioration accelerates markedly after 48 hours; staling for 96 hours can cause 7.4–17.0% cane weight loss and ~2.0% sugar recovery loss.\n- Average Indian mill-level loss: 10–15 kg sugar per tonne of cane crushed, due to time lag alone.\n- Moisture loss rate: 1.5–2% per 24 hours after cutting.\n- Best-practice window for processing: within 24–48 hours of cutting, per multiple cross-referenced sources.\n\n- Given Karnataka's high per-season irrigation requirement (32 irrigations, 266 kg water per kg sugar produced), farmers in the state should prioritize migration from furrow irrigation to drip irrigation, which the source data shows can lower net water requirement from ~1,878 mm/year to ~1,186 mm/year.\n- During monsoon disruptions, adopt the contingency schedule appropriate to the delay: for a 2-week delayed monsoon onset, continue pre-monsoon irrigation intervals (8–10 days) with alternate-furrow application and trash mulching in ratoon; for longer delays (6–8 weeks), shift to a 10-day protective irrigation interval and watch for iron deficiency, which becomes likely under prolonged dry spells (foliar spray of 1% FeSO₄ + 0.5% ZnSO₄ + 2% urea is recommended in that case).", "farmer": "Is my soil too dry right now?"}, "retrieval_methods": {"original": "Is my soil too dry right now?", "sarvam": "Is my soil too dry?", "groq": "soil moisture level sugarcane dry?"}, "retrieval_best_by_method": {"original": 0.8368, "sarvam": 0.8296, "groq": 0.8792}, "retrieved": [{"source": "nutrient_deficiency/01_iron_chlorosis_and_micronutrient_deficiency.md", "section": "Nutrient Deficiency Management in Sugarcane (Iron Chlorosis and Micronutrients) — Main Information", "score": 0.8765, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "sugarcane/01_sugarcane_cultivation_overview_india_and_karnataka.md", "section": "Sugarcane Cultivation in India: Agro-Climatic Overview (with Karnataka Focus) — Main Information", "score": 0.8792, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "harvesting/02_post_harvest_management_and_sucrose_loss.md", "section": "Post-Harvest Management of Sugarcane: Preventing Sucrose Loss — Key Facts", "score": 0.8695, "via": "groq"}, {"source": "irrigation/01_irrigation_water_management.md", "section": "Sugarcane Irrigation Water Management — Important Recommendations", "score": 0.8726, "via": "groq"}], "raw_answer": "No problem! This isn't about whether you have enough moisture — it's just how much rain fell this month compared to what was normal over three years combined. If there were no significant changes since last year or if your current situation matches one documented as having good yield potential without any specific reason why it shouldn't be fine yet, then I think we need more information before making judgments on actual risk here.", "claims": ["unsupport